when we think of surfboards  we usually picture brightly colored  lengths of foam and fiberglass composite  so it comes as a surprise to see a  wooden surfboard but why after all every  kind of waterborne vessel was once made  from trees that's because no matter what  wood floats two planks of poplar are  glued on either side of a strip of oak  using a template a craftsman draws the  shape he wants for the surfboard with a  Japanese pole saw he cuts the shape out  by hand establishing the basic form of  the surfboard a CNC router quickly and  accurately cuts the inner support  framework for the board from poplar  plywood the builder sources wood from  suppliers who use sustainable forestry  practices  using his pole saw the craftsman  carefully freezed the pieces of the  support framework and removes them  before smoothing the edges with a block  sander he then begins fitting the pieces  together to form the framework for the  spine the Builder uses fast drawing  superglue which bonds powerfully and  quickly allowing him to work fast with  the spine complete he can start gluing  and inserting the ribs the finished  frame resembles the skeleton of a large  fish once the support structure has  cured the craftsman places it on the  bottom skin of the surfboard he  positions it carefully and clamped it  securely in place on a purpose-built jig  he then traces the lines of the frame  onto the skin while measuring and  marking a set distance from the end of  each rib  he squeezes a bead of an epoxy based  glue over the network of lines before  lifting the frame into position on the  skin he clamps everything down and  leaves it to cure overnight the sides of  the surfboard which are called the rails  consists of 12 strips of wood glued and  clamped together layer by layer this  process takes several days  when all of the glue is fully cured the  builder lays a bead of polyurethane glue  on the frame and rails he then lowers  the surfboards top skin into position  and secures it with dozens of clamps he  will let the fully assembled board cure  overnight  once it is cured the board is ready for  the final phase of production the  craftsman must shape and smooth the wood  to give the object its final shape  the rails act as cutting guides as he  uses a pole saw to trim off the skin  where it overlaps them  with a small Japanese maid wood plane he  begins to round the top edges of the  board clients with a DIY philosophy can  sign up for workshops at this facility  in which they learn to make their own  surfboard the builder also uses a  special tool called a saw ASP a square  ensures he starts from the same point on  the bottom and top when shaping the nose  and tail the saw ASP  shapes the wood quickly and efficiently  now that the surfboard has its final  shape the craftsman uses a sanding block  with increasingly finer grits of  sandpaper to create a smooth surface he  signs the bottom of the board with a  pencil before burning it into the wood  with a tool called a pyrography pen  a layer of fiberglass and epoxy gives  the wood a tough outer shell customers  can choose to have a thin box with a  removable fin or they can opt for a  permanent wooden fin traditional  material plus an innovative design  results in wooden surfboards that are  built to last
 

